            <ArticleTitle>A Case of Familial Partial Lipodystrophy: From Clinical Phenotype to Genetics.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>376-378</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1499-2671(15)30020-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcjd.2015.12.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We discuss the case of a 56-year-old woman who presented with diabetes from the age of 24. The diagnosis of familial partial lipodystrophy was made after the discovery of the lamin A/C gene 20 years later. The diagnosis enabled the detection of a severe cardiac rhythm disorder with the need for an implantable defibrillator. This disease is a rare disorder characterized by an altered body fat repartition, cardiac rhythm anomalies and muscular dystrophy.</AbstractText>
